# 5️⃣ PROBABILITY DISTRIBUTIONS ⭐⭐⭐

A distribution describes:

> **How values occur across possible outcomes.**

This is one of the most important statistical concepts for ML.

---

## Normal Distribution

The famous bell curve:

```text
                 ▲
               ███
             ███████
           ███████████
        █████████████████
───────┴───────────────────┴──────
              Mean
```

Characteristics:

* symmetric
* mean ≈ median ≈ mode
* many natural measurements approximately follow it

Examples can include:

* measurement errors
* some biological measurements
* standardized test-related data

But be careful:

> **Not every real-world dataset is normally distributed.**

---

# 6️⃣ THE 68–95–99.7 RULE

For an approximately normal distribution:

```text
Mean ± 1 SD → ~68%
Mean ± 2 SD → ~95%
Mean ± 3 SD → ~99.7%
```

Visual:

```text
             68%
          ┌────────┐
       ┌──┴────────┴──┐
      95%
   ┌──┴────────────────┴──┐
 99.7%
┌─┴────────────────────────┴─┐
```

This is useful for understanding:

* unusual observations
* outliers
* standardized data
* model assumptions

---

# 7️⃣ SKEWNESS

You saw distributions yesterday. Now let's understand them statistically.

## Symmetric

```text
        /\
       /  \
      /    \
_____/______\_____
```

Mean and median are relatively close.

---

## Right-skewed

```text
       /\
      /  \
     /    \____________
____/___________________
```

Long tail on the right.

Examples can include:

```text
Income
House prices
Transaction amounts
```

Typically:

```text
Mean > Median
```

---

## Left-skewed

```text
____________
          /\
         /  \
________/____\____
```

Typically:

```text
Mean < Median
```

