# 9️⃣ BIAS–VARIANCE INTUITION ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

This is one of the **most important ML concepts** today.

Imagine you want to predict house prices.

---

## High Bias

Your model is too simple.

```text
Reality:
↗ ↘ ↗ ↘ ↗

Model:
────────────
```

It cannot capture the real relationship.

This is:

> **Underfitting**

Symptoms:

```text
Training error → high
Validation error → high
```

---

# High Variance

Your model is too complicated.

```text
Training data:

•──•──•──•──•
 \  /\_/\/
```

The model memorizes training data instead of learning general patterns.

This is:

> **Overfitting**

Symptoms:

```text
Training error → very low
Validation/test error → high
```

---

# 🎯 The Goal

We want a model that captures the real underlying pattern.

```text
Too simple
    ↓
High Bias
    ↓
GOOD MODEL
    ↓
High Variance
    ↓
Too complex
```

This is the famous:

> **Bias–Variance Tradeoff**

---

# 🔥 REAL-WORLD EXAMPLE

Imagine you're building a model to predict whether a customer will cancel a subscription.

Features:

```text
Age
Monthly bill
Login frequency
Support tickets
Subscription length
```

### Model 1

Very simple:

```text
if monthly_bill > 100:
    churn = yes
```

Too simplistic.

Potentially:

```text
High Bias
```

---

### Model 2

Extremely complex:

```text
Age + bill + every login + every transaction
+ every tiny behavior...
```

It may memorize the training customers.

Potentially:

```text
High Variance
```

---

### Model 3

Learns the important patterns while generalizing to unseen customers.

That's what we want.

